\section*{Question 2}

Let \(f\) be the function defined for \(x \geq 0\) with \(f(0)=5\) and \(f^{\prime}\), the first derivative of \(f\), given by \(f^{\prime}(x)=e^{(-x / 4)} \sin \left(x^{2}\right)\). The graph of \(y=f^{\prime}(x)\) is shown above.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] Use the graph of \(f^{\prime}\) to determine whether the graph of \(f\) is concave up, concave down, or neither on the interval \(1.7<x<1.9\). Explain your reasoning.
\item[(b)] On the interval \(0 \leq x \leq 3\), find the value of \(x\) at which \(f\) has an absolute maximum. Justify your answer.
\end{itemize}
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/f4043484-5954-4a94-a119-dd43cb7b024d-1.jpg?height=496&width=679&top_left_y=303&top_left_x=1259)
\begin{itemize}
\item[(c)] Write an equation for the line tangent to the graph of \(f\) at \(x=2\).
\item[(a)] On the interval \(1.7<x<1.9, f^{\prime}\) is decreasing and thus \(f\) is concave down on this interval.
\item[(b)] \(f^{\prime}(x)=0\) when \(x=0, \sqrt{\pi}, \sqrt{2 \pi}, \sqrt{3 \pi}, \ldots\) On \([0,3] f^{\prime}\) changes from positive to negative only at \(\sqrt{\pi}\). The absolute maximum must occur at \(x=\sqrt{\pi}\) or at an endpoint.
\[
\begin{aligned}
& f(0)=5 \\
& f(\sqrt{\pi})=f(0)+\int_{0}^{\sqrt{\pi}} f^{\prime}(x) d x=5.67911 \\
& f(3)=f(0)+\int_{0}^{3} f^{\prime}(x) d x=5.57893
\end{aligned}
\]
This shows that \(f\) has an absolute maximum at \(x=\sqrt{\pi}\).
\item[(c)] \(f(2)=f(0)+\int_{0}^{2} f^{\prime}(x) d x=5.62342\)
\[
\begin{aligned}
